{
  "name": "How to Plan Your Wheelchair Accessible Home Remodel",
  "step": [
    {
      "name": "Step 1",
      "text": "Planning a wheelchair accessible home remodel is all about making sure every space works for you. It might seem daunting, but we can simplify it. First off, you'll want to measure those doorways and hallways; can your wheelchair comfortably pass through? Don't forget about obstructions like furniture or tight corners. Next, look at your entryways and any thresholds – those little bumps can be big barriers. Bathrooms are huge for accessibility, so evaluate obstacles there. Think about things like roll-in showers, open knee-space vanities, and grab bars. When it comes to stairs, consider ramps or stair chairs/lifts. Offset hinges can even widen existing doorways without major structural changes. If you’re dealing with multiple levels or specific clearance needs, that’s when a qualified accessibility professional or occupational therapist can really shine. They ensure everything is planned to precision. If you’re ready to get started, give HH Remodeling LLC a call!",
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 1
    }
  ],
